== Crash matrix: real child process killed at each point, 20 repetitions each
   
   crash point            | expected | got      | recovery action             | pass
   -----------------------+----------+----------+-----------------------------+-----
   torn_pointer           | A        | A x20    | rolled_back                 | PASS
   before_pointer_rename  | A        | A x20    | rolled_back                 | PASS
   after_pointer_rename   | B        | B x20    | rolled_forward              | PASS
   after_commit           | B        | B x20    | nothing open                | PASS
   
   torn_pointer           half a pointer in the temp file; rename never happened
   before_pointer_rename  temp pointer complete + fsynced; rename never happened
   after_pointer_rename   rename happened; directory not yet fsynced
   after_commit           COMMIT record written; GC not yet run
   (80 child processes in 14.0s)
   PASS matrix torn_pointer: 20/20 runs recovered to exactly A  - A x20
   PASS matrix before_pointer_rename: 20/20 runs recovered to exactly A  - A x20
   PASS matrix after_pointer_rename: 20/20 runs recovered to exactly B  - B x20
   PASS matrix after_commit: 20/20 runs recovered to exactly B  - B x20

== Power loss at the same points (SimFS: unflushed writes / directory entries may be lost)
   A process kill keeps the page cache; a power cut does not. SimFS records every
   system call of put(B) as micro-ops and enumerates every disk state a power cut
   could leave (ALICE model), then runs the REAL recovery code on each one.
   
   model W1 - atomic rename (ext4/xfs/btrfs/NTFS)
     syscalls around the commit point:
       k=12  create .CURRENT.28668.aead3a05.tmp
       k=14  write 91B .CURRENT.28668.aead3a05.tmp
       k=15  fsync .CURRENT.28668.aead3a05.tmp
       k=16  rename .CURRENT.28668.aead3a05.tmp -> CURRENT    <-- COMMIT POINT
       k=17  fsync dir doc
     cut just before the CURRENT rename            2 distinct states (exhaustive): A=2       must be A
   PASS SimFS W1: cut just before the CURRENT rename -> only A  - A=2
     cut just after the rename (dir not flushed)   3 distinct states (exhaustive): A=2 B=1   must be A or B
   PASS SimFS W1: cut just after the rename (dir not flushed) -> only A/B  - A=2 B=1
     cut after the directory fsync                 1 distinct states (exhaustive): B=1       must be B
   PASS SimFS W1: cut after the directory fsync -> only B  - B=1
   
   model W0 - worst case: rename = delete dst, delete src, insert dst
     syscalls around the commit point:
       k=13  create .CURRENT.28668.019032f1.tmp
       k=15  write 91B .CURRENT.28668.019032f1.tmp
       k=16  fsync .CURRENT.28668.019032f1.tmp
       k=19  rename .CURRENT.28668.019032f1.tmp -> CURRENT    <-- COMMIT POINT
       k=20  fsync dir doc
     cut just before the CURRENT rename            2 distinct states (exhaustive): A=2       must be A
   PASS SimFS W0: cut just before the CURRENT rename -> only A  - A=2
     cut just after the rename (dir not flushed)   6 distinct states (exhaustive): A=2 B=4   must be A or B
   PASS SimFS W0: cut just after the rename (dir not flushed) -> only A/B  - A=2 B=4
     cut after the directory fsync                 1 distinct states (exhaustive): B=1       must be B
   PASS SimFS W0: cut after the directory fsync -> only B  - B=1

== Assumptions
   - controlled failure = os._exit(137) in a real child process at a named point
     (ATOMICDOCS_CRASH_AT); the OS keeps the page cache, so this is a process crash;
   - local file system with atomic same-directory rename (NTFS here; ext4/xfs/APFS);
   - power loss is simulated (SimFS), not a real power cut; drive honours flushes.
   total time 14.8s

